\begin{abstract}
We present the STME-RS-A framework, a warped extra-dimensional model incorporating 
quartic bulk anharmonicity. The resulting Kaluza--Klein (KK) spectrum exhibits 
non-equidistant level spacing, providing a falsifiable geometric signature distinct 
from standard Randall--Sundrum constructions. We further integrate this geometry 
into a full-scale propulsion simulation architecture, including CFD solvers, 
finite-rate chemistry, thermodynamic coupling, and multi-threaded stress testing 
under extreme chamber pressure and thermal loads. Benchmark points (BP1--BP4) 
and stress-test results at \SI{3}{ab^{-1}} HL-LHC conditions are provided.
\end{abstract}

% -------------------------------------------------
\section{Introduction}
Warped extra-dimensional models offer a geometric solution to the hierarchy problem. 
Standard Randall--Sundrum (RS) frameworks assume harmonic bulk dynamics, producing 
nearly equidistant KK spectra. The STME-RS-A model extends this construction by 
introducing a quartic anharmonic term in the bulk scalar potential, yielding 
modified Sturm--Liouville eigenvalues and distinctive collider signatures.

In parallel, we integrate this geometry into a high-fidelity propulsion simulation 
environment capable of resolving multi-species combustion, shock formation, 
thermal loading, and structural stress under extreme operating conditions.

% -------------------------------------------------
\section{The STME-RS-A Framework}

\subsection{Warped Geometry}
The five-dimensional metric is
\begin{equation}
ds^2 = e^{-2k|y|}\eta_{\mu\nu}dx^\mu dx^\nu + dy^2,
\end{equation}
with curvature scale $k$ and compactification interval $kL$.

\subsection{Bulk Scalar Potential}
The scalar field $\Phi$ obeys the anharmonic potential
\begin{equation}
V(\Phi) = \lambda \Phi^2 + \lambda_4 \Phi^4,
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_4$ introduces controlled deviations from harmonic confinement.

% -------------------------------------------------
\section{Kaluza--Klein Mass Spectrum}

The KK mass eigenvalues follow
\begin{equation}
m_n^2 = 4k^2 
+ 2\sqrt{\lambda}\left(n+\tfrac{1}{2}\right)
+ \frac{\lambda_4}{16\lambda^2}\left(6n^2 + 6n + 3\right),
\end{equation}
yielding non-equidistant spacing:
\begin{equation}
\frac{m_2}{m_1} \neq 2, \qquad 
\frac{m_3}{m_2} \neq \frac{m_2}{m_1}.
\end{equation}

